        "title": "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e4fe4f6f-f6af-5453-84cb-faeac40a08d5",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1.$\n\nThe slope at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={e}^{3x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(x)^{\\prime}\\cdot (e^{3 x})-(x)\\cdot (e^{3 x})^{\\prime}}{(e^{3 x})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{3x}-x\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}}{{({e}^{3x})}^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{{e}^{3x}(1-3x)}{{({e}^{3x})}^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{1-3x}{{e}^{3x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}\\,(1)&=\\frac{1-3(1)}{{e}^{3(1)}}\\\\ &=\\frac{-2}{{e}^{3}}\\\\ &=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at $x=1$ is $-\\frac{2}{{e}^{3}}.$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
